  "id": "DrugBank_DB00974 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Edetic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypercalcemia Disease through its role in the phosphorus metabolism pathway. The evidence suggests that Edetic Acid is associated with Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, which in turn is linked to Hypercalcemia Disease via three distinct pathways involving different calcium compounds—Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glycerophosphate, and Calcium Glucoheptonate. These compounds are all involved in the regulation of calcium homeostasis, and their involvement in Hypercalcemia Disease indicates a potential mechanism by which Edetic Acid may influence calcium levels. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust, mechanistically plausible link, though the exact mechanism by which Edetic Acid affects calcium metabolism remains to be elucidated. The presence of multiple calcium compounds in the pathway highlights the complexity of calcium regulation and the potential for Edetic Acid to modulate this process through different routes.",
